import {texEq as eq,inlineMath} from './math.js';
import {section,table,cite} from './topic-tools.js';
const report=()=>cite('https://huggingface.co/deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash/blob/main/DeepSeek_V41_Tech_Report.pdf','DeepSeek V4.1 report');
const comparison=(caption,heads,rows)=>table(caption,heads,rows).replace('<div class="dense-table-wrap">','<div class="dense-table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable DeepSeek comparison">');
export function deepseekMemory(link){return section('deepseek-memory','DeepSeek: separate local state, shared KV and lookup memory',`<p>The causal encoder–decoder changes a dependency, not merely a kernel. In an ordinary stack, each layer needs the preceding layer’s prompt representations to build its cache. Here the decoder projects global memory from final encoder states. Future tokens remain inaccessible: the encoder is causal, not bidirectional.</p>
${eq(String.raw`C_\ell=H_{20}W^{KV}_\ell,\qquad Z_\ell=H_{20}W^Z_\ell`,'Row-vector convention: H₂₀ contains final encoder states at model width 5,120. C supplies decoder KV entries; Z supplies compression weights. Shared CSA2 modes reduce the number of distinct caches actually constructed.')}
<div class="dense-flow" role="img" aria-label="Prompt passes through twenty causal encoder blocks; final encoder states create decoder global KV and a short replay tail. Generated tokens still execute all forty blocks."><div>Prompt tokens</div><span>→</span><div>20 causal encoder blocks</div><span>→</span><div>Encoder states<br>Decoder global KV</div><span>+</span><div>128-position<br>decoder-tail replay</div></div>
<p>Each decoder layer still needs its own sliding-window state. Serving replays the last 128 encoder positions through the decoder, restricting local attention to that segment. It does not persist decoder SWA state between prefills. Encoder prefix-cache reuse likewise reconstructs local state from a bounded tail while retaining global state. Stacked local layers can depend on positions before the replay segment. This approximation is simulated during post-training; a reported small quality effect does not eliminate uncharacterized edge cases.</p>
${comparison('Three CSA2 modes; every mode keeps its own main query and local window',['Mode','Global KV / indexer keys','Sparse selection'],[['Full','Construct','Fresh'],['Reindex','Reuse','Fresh query and selection'],['Reuse','Reuse','Reuse latest selection']])}
<pre tabindex="0" aria-label="DeepSeek attention block layout"><code>Encoder: 2 SWA-only + 3 × [Full, Reuse, Reuse, Reuse, Reuse, Reuse]
         Global compression ratio 2
Decoder: [Full, Reuse, Reuse, Reuse] + 4 × [Reindex, Reuse, Reuse, Reuse]
         Global compression ratio 1; still sparse selection
Every layer retains its own 128-position local attention path.</code></pre>
<p>Compared with earlier CSA, compression uses nonoverlapping groups of m tokens, rather than overlapping groups of 2m. It removes the compressor’s absolute-position component. Indexer keys come from main KV instead of an independently compressed path.</p>
${comparison('V4.1 attention and expert dimensions from the report',['Component','Configuration'],[['Main attention','64 query heads; head dimension 512; query compression 1,280'],['Output projection','8 groups; intermediate dimension 1,024'],['Sparse indexer','32 heads × 128; top 512 entries'],['Decoder candidate pool','Up to 2,048 blocks × 8 positions = 16,384'],['MoE in all 40 blocks','384 routed / 6 selected / 1 shared; intermediate width 2,304'],['Expert activation','SwiGLU, clamped at 10'],['Single-Pass mHC','4 streams; 20 Sinkhorn-Knopp iterations']])}
<p>The first decoder Full layer scans the global context and chooses its own top 512 entries plus the shared candidate pool. Later Reindex layers choose 512 only within that pool. The initial scan still grows with sequence length. Excluded evidence cannot reappear downstream. The hierarchy is introduced during post-training, not only at deployment.</p>
<p>Main global KV uses E2M1 values and one E4M3 scale per sixteen channels, without NVFP4’s second-level global scale. Quantization occurs after RoPE and uses post-training QAT. More sensitive local SWA cache stays FP8. Global values are dequantized before attention; this is not a claim of native FP4 attention matrix multiplication. The reported 890 bytes per input token becomes 890 MB decimal, about 0.83 GiB, at one million tokens. That excludes local caches, activations, workspaces and model weights.</p>
<p>Engram adds a different memory. Two modules at zero-indexed layers 1 and 14 split 196B parameters evenly. Each uses orders 2, 3 and 4, eight hash heads and total embedding width 2,048 per order. Heads address roughly 16M entries with distinct prime table sizes. Tokenizer compression, hashing, context-aware gates and residual integration remain; the short causal convolution is omitted because its measured benefit did not justify serving complexity.</p>
<p>Tables and key/value projections use FP8. Deterministic addresses permit background RDMA prefetch from host memory when serving. RL rollouts instead keep tables on GPU to reduce host pressure and fragmentation failures. These placements are not interchangeable memory estimates.</p>
${eq(String.raw`\frac{(2n+2)d}{(4n+4)d}=\frac12`,'Single-Pass mHC shifts input-mixing coefficients by one block. This ratio covers the stated activation-traffic component, not all model traffic or end-to-end speed.')}
<p>The serving Mega-mHC kernel fuses residual update, input mixing, coefficient prediction, pre-normalization and FP8 conversion. Pretraining keeps multiple kernels with the same changed coefficient dependency. <a href="${link('models/attention')}#sparse">Attention →</a>, <a href="${link('models/recurrence')}">Conditional Memory →</a> and <a href="${link('execution/serving')}">Serving →</a> explain the connected operators. ${report()} §§2–3 · ${cite('https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.05254','YOCO antecedent')}.</p>`);}
export function deepseekRun(link){return section('deepseek-run','DeepSeek: retain the data and optimizer contract',`<p>The 45T exposure has a 7:1 text-only/multimodal token ratio. This is neither a document ratio nor a count of unique tokens. Scaling experiments vary data and model size. Domain experts define finer quality criteria. Filtering targets low-information generated text, weak-model outputs and poor machine translations, not every form of synthetic data. Coding adds recent repositories, commits, libraries, frameworks and languages; exact language/code allocations remain undisclosed.</p>
<p>Image–alt-text relevance filtering and semantic image deduplication complement interleaved webpages and PDFs. Grounding, pointing, OCR, image–code pairs and computer-use trajectories supply other tasks. Cheap document checks precede costly image retrieval. Image-aware filtering and SmolVLM scoring follow. Some rejected interleaved documents become image–text pairs. Renewed crawling addresses the text bias of existing Common Crawl material.</p>
<p>Cross-representation deduplication replaces matching text with its multimodal version and retains the larger configured epoch count. Pretraining and context-extension allocation are coordinated to reduce overlap. Deterministic splitting and best-fit packing keep reported padding at or below 10⁻⁴. <a href="${link('training/data')}">Training Data →</a> owns the reusable pipeline.</p>
${comparison('DeepSeek run settings; stages and parameter classes differ',['Setting','Reported value'],[['Global batch / exposure','100.6M tokens / 45T'],['Context','Sparse attention from scratch at 64K; 1M at 34T exposure'],['Learning rate','2,000-step warmup; 2.6×10⁻⁴ until 28T; cosine to 2.6×10⁻⁵ by 40T; constant to 45T'],['Muon','Momentum 0.95; weight decay 0.1; update RMS 0.18'],['AdamW',inlineMath(String.raw`\beta_1=0.9`)+ '; '+inlineMath(String.raw`\beta_2=0.95`)+'; '+inlineMath(String.raw`\epsilon=10^{-20}`)+'; weight decay 0.1'],['Engram learning rate','5× multiplier'],['Modality bias update / sequence loss','0.001 / 0.0001'],['Table/embedding/head optimizer','Nesterov; 11 Sinkhorn balancing iterations; '+inlineMath(String.raw`\tau=10^{-3}`)+'; '+inlineMath(String.raw`\epsilon=10^{-20}`)+'; no weight decay']])}
<p>Muon updates backbone matrices, Engram projections and the vision-language projector. Q/K weights receive head-wise preconditioning. Nonmatrix parameters use AdamW. The table/embedding/head optimizer balances squared update magnitudes with alternating row/column scaling. It replaces Newton–Schulz on updates, not the forward embedding. Retaining momentum without Adam’s second-moment buffer matters for a 196B table. This optimizer operation is distinct from forward mHC Sinkhorn-Knopp mixing.</p>
<p>The vision encoder has 32 layers, width 1,024, sixteen heads, 14-pixel patches, 2D RoPE, RMSNorm and SwiGLU. Linear patch projection suits Muon. A 3×3 pixel unshuffle moves nine patches into channels before a two-layer width-5,120 bridge. Maximum image resolution is approximately 1,344×1,344. The earlier vision-training section keeps bootstrap, auxiliary 4B alignment, transfer and later unfreezing separate.</p>
<p>Shared attention crosses pipeline boundaries. Shadow indexers retain one logical parameter owner, synchronized weights and gradients, explicit KV/index payloads and microbatch-scoped lifetimes. Engram tables are row-sharded across dedicated groups; optimizer state is further sharded across replicas. Deterministic lookup allows prefetch before a microbatch. Vision executes separately from language forward/backward; large images are balanced over context ranks and loaded once.</p>
<p>At a constant 100.6M-token batch, 45T corresponds to about 447,000 steps. That arithmetic does not disclose GPU-hours or calendar time. <a href="${link('training/optimization')}">Optimization →</a> and <a href="${link('training/parallelism')}">Distributed Training →</a> explain update and ownership contracts. ${report()} §§3–4.</p>`);}
export function deepseekPost(link){return `
${section('deepseek-factory','DeepSeek: build a task that survives independent checks',`<p>A task joins a problem, executable environment and verifier. A plausible instruction is insufficient if the container is broken or the test rewards the wrong behavior. Constructors receive difficulty and correctness feedback. New solver trajectories trigger fresh audits when tasks enter another RL run.</p>
<ol class="dense-checklist"><li><b>Collect.</b> Voluntary employee/partner sessions are filtered for difficulty or weak performance, then deduplicated by trajectory. GitHub repositories above an unspecified star threshold provide another source.</li><li><b>Plan.</b> Check buildability and automatic verification. Select a turn or commit, propose substantial implementation work, define target and regression tests, document required external resources.</li><li><b>Build.</b> Install dependencies, prepare the tree, write instructions/tests, self-test, remove solution leakage and package a new container-image layer.</li><li><b>Solve independently.</b> Several agents expose ambiguity, unsolvability, difficulty and possible exploits.</li><li><b>Inspect independently.</b> Check setup, factual consistency, instruction/test agreement and hackable scoring against both artifacts and trajectories.</li><li><b>Repair and recalibrate.</b> Fix defects and adjust tasks that are too easy or too hard. Repeat construction/validation when needed; later trajectories feed re-audits.</li></ol>
<div class="dense-flow" role="img" aria-label="Task construction separates planner, builder, independent solvers, inspector and repair; accepted tasks later return for re-audit"><div>Planner</div><span>→</span><div>Builder</div><span>→</span><div>Independent<br>solvers</div><span>→</span><div>Inspector</div><span>→</span><div>Repair<br>and re-audit</div></div>
<p>Fail-to-pass checks cover the requested repair; pass-to-pass checks protect existing behavior. Starting from a commit does not restrict construction to replaying its reference patch. The planner can synthesize new work. Generated tests can still be inconsistent or exploitable, which motivates separation of responsibilities.</p>
<p>General-agent synthesis reconstructs voluntary workflows, schemas, constraints and tool behavior for SaaS, enterprise and specialized backends. Negative feedback seeds single- and multi-turn failure situations. Mock tools require fidelity validation; matching an API schema alone does not reproduce a real service’s failures. <a href="${link('post-training/agents')}">Agent Training →</a> and <a href="${link('training/frontier-recipes')}#glm-run">GLM’s environment recipe →</a> provide connected mechanisms, not a claim that V4.1 invented each pattern.</p>
<p>DSec targets millions of concurrent instances. Scale units contain failures and resource exhaustion. Independent schedulers estimate resources with relaxed global consistency; each node makes the final admission decision. Worker VMs bind to sub-NUMA domains, with containers using local CPU/memory. The reported increase from about 1,000 to over 2,500 live containers per node is workload-specific, not simultaneous repository builds.</p>
<p>Latency-sensitive classes stay separate from SCHED_IDLE background jobs. Core scheduling prevents different priorities sharing sibling hyperthreads. AppArmor profiles and fine-grained eBPF network policies limit interference and leakage. Sandbox crashes count as failed trajectories and emit training feedback. Package services and infrastructure remain part of the verifier’s attack surface.</p>
<p>Claude Code, OpenCode, Pi and DeepSeek Harness variants expose different interfaces and versions. A trajectory worker uses a common training schema; tools and environment state live outside the preemptible GPU pool. GPUs alternate rollout and learning, interrupting generation at token boundaries. Completed samples free dispatch capacity without waiting for the same earlier group. Group-relative advantages still belong to one prompt.</p>
<p>Resumed trajectories can reuse KV and recorded expert routes under newer weights. Concatenated routing replay retains each segment’s choices; it does not regenerate history with the latest checkpoint. Dataset concurrency and optional short-early-return rejection address completion-time bias. Dispatch/waiting bounds and excessively stale-token masks address policy age. The undisclosed thresholds must not become invented universal defaults.</p>
${eq(String.raw`\begin{aligned}R_{\rm length}&=-\min\!\left(C_{\max},k(b)\frac{N_{\rm reason}}{L_{\rm norm}}\right)\\k(b)&=k_0\exp\!\left(-\frac{b-b_{\min}}{\tau}\right)\end{aligned}`,'b ranges from 1 to 100. Cmax caps the deduction; Lnorm is reference length; k₀ and τ set brevity pressure. Require positive Lnorm and τ. Higher effort weakens the penalty.')}
<p>Center rewards within the same problem and effort subgroup, not across low/high responses. API presets are 50, 75 and 100; interpolation is not an inference hard-token limit. Intermediate model merging initializes successive RL runs, with its exact rule undisclosed. Final full-vocabulary on-policy distillation consolidates over forty heterogeneous teachers on student-visited prefixes. Teacher/domain allocation, mixture and concurrency can change with evaluations. The complete objective is not disclosed; this is distinct from teacher-trace SFT and intermediate parameter merging. ${report()} §5.</p>`)}
${section('deepseek-evidence','DeepSeek: compare the same benchmark and expose missing costs',`<p>DSpark has three blocks, five parallel draft positions, a local window of 128 and a lightweight Markov head for draft dependencies. A confidence head estimates acceptance; scheduling combines those estimates with engine throughput profiles. Backbone pretraining omits MTP. A separate frozen-backbone stage trains DSpark, then post-training updates the drafter without sending its loss into backbone weights. Serving and RL/OPD rollouts use it. <a href="${link('execution/speculation')}">Speculative Decoding →</a> owns the broader implementation.</p>
${comparison('Technical report Table 3, maximum effort; task metrics are not interchangeable',['Benchmark / metric','V4-Flash','V4.1-Flash'],[['GPQA Diamond, pass@1','89.9','90.9'],['Terminal-Bench 2.1, pass@1','82.7','90.6'],['Terminal-Bench 3.0, pass@1','7.6','30.0'],['Terminal-Bench 4.0, pass@1','7.0','31.2'],['DeepSWE v1.1, resolved','54.4','74.2'],['NL2Repo-Bench, score','54.2','65.4'],['AutomationBench, pass@1','37.7','54.8'],["Agents’ Last Exam, pass@1",'25.2','31.8']])}
<p>The original release check found NL2Repo 64.0 on the model card versus 65.4 in the report. These are separate artifact values. Terminal-Bench 2.1’s high result must not obscure much lower absolute results on versions 3 and 4. ZeroBench 49.0 is pass@5, not pass@1. Native multimodality is not evidence of winning every visual task.</p>
${comparison('Same V4.1 checkpoint, different DeepSWE harnesses',['Harness','Resolved rate'],[['mini-SWE','74.2'],['DeepSeek Harness Minimal','72.6'],['Claude Code','69.8'],['Codex','65.6']])}
<p>Coding generally uses temperature 1.0, top-p 0.95 and 1M context; visual-agent evaluation uses a different setup including 512K. Network restrictions, removed Git histories and build/package-cache cleanup reduce leakage. Exploit-seeking behavior remains. These are author-reported results, not independent reproduction.</p>
<p>The report attributes post-training gains mainly to tasks, environments, verification and scale; it does not claim a new core RL algorithm or isolate every component. CED builds on YOCO, Single-Pass mixing on mHC, and memory-update balancing on earlier momentum/balancing methods. Architecture and engineering co-design are useful without implying that every ingredient is new.</p>
<p>Missing reproduction details include SFT counts, exact mixture/filter yields/masks, unique RL tasks and environments, accepted-task rate and construction cost, per-task CPU/RAM, node inventory, GPU-hours and calendar time. Complete reward coefficients, group sizes, staleness thresholds, domain schedules, merge rules and teacher settings also remain unspecified. Millions of live containers are not millions of tasks; 45T pretraining tokens are not an RL budget.</p>
<p>Reference inference code is not the production serving stack. Its straightforward all-layer path and out-of-scope speculative loop do not reproduce production CED skipping, bounded replay or DSpark scheduling. Verify actual execution paths before benchmarking those claims. <a href="${link('execution/serving')}">Serving Models →</a> connects cache ownership and prefill/decode separation. ${report()} §§5.3–6 · ${cite('https://huggingface.co/deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash','Model card')} · ${cite('https://huggingface.co/deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash/tree/main/inference','Reference implementation')}</p>`)}
`;}
